<head>
  <title>AdapRas: Raster fitting</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>
<body>
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->


  <p>The operation in AdapRas is made based on the relation of the three parameters described below:  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <li>Side</li>
    <li>Net</li>
    <li>Horizontal reference system</li>
  </blockquote>

    <p>If the sides, net and horizontal reference systems are equal, an adaptation is made to the envelope of the second raster. The values of the result will be the same as those of the raster to be adapted, except those of the area of the raster that does not have any point on the raster to be adapted, in this case the values will be equal to NoData. Any resampling is done.</p>

    <p>If the reference systems are the same, but the sides and/or the nets are different, a fitting by densification of rasters is performed, according to one of the four resampling methods: nearest neighbor, 4 neighbors mode, bilinear interpolation or bicubic interpolation.</p>

    <p>If the geographic reference systems are different, a system change is made (projection parameters, datum). In this case, a resampling will be done according to one of the four methods: nearest neighbor, 4 neighbors mode, bilinear interpolation or bicubic interpolation.</p>

  <p>The NoData value will be equal, as appropriate, to the value given by the user, if not beyond the range of raster data representation to adapt or, alternatively, the present value of the raster to adapt or, however, if this is not defined, an implicit value that depends on the type of raster data to adapt as detailed below.</p>

  <div align="center">
    <table border="2" bordercolor="#000000" width="332">
      <tr>
        <th scope="col" width="162">Data Type</th>

        <th scope="col" width="160"><b>NoData Value</b></th>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>bit (logical)</td>

        <td>0</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>byte (character)</td>

        <td>255</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>integer (enter)</td>

        <td>-32768</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>long</td>

        <td>-2147483648</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>real</td>

        <td>-3.4E+38</td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </div>


  <p>RECOMMENDATIONS SUMMARY (where <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a> program is indicated also applies to <a href="AdapRas.htm">AdapRas</a>AdapRas)
  <pre>
EXPANSION:  + Categorical map:   CanviRes [followed by the method in X. Pons' Ph D thesis]
            + Sensor image:      CanviRes for classification (not recommended)
                                 DensRas L, for visual analysis
            + DEM:               DensRas L

CONTRACTION:+ Categorical map:   CanviRes, M option
            + Sensor image:      CanviRes, C option for classification
                                 CanviRes, d or m option for visual analysis
            + DEM:               CanviRes, d or m option for median or mean heights
                                 DensRas L, for heights that prioritize central values in the resulting cell
</pre><hr/>  <exclusiva>
